1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,120
昨晚六点半，北京上空
Last night at 6:30, over Beijing,

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,610
一个大火球拖着绿光往下砸。
a huge fireball fell trailing green light.

3
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:06,820
河北、山西的网友也拍到了，
People in Hebei and Shanxi filmed it too;

4
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,310
天文馆专家说：多半是同一颗。
planetarium experts say it was probably the same one.

5
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:10,100
一颗流星，
One meteor,

6
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,320
照亮了三个省。
lighting up three provinces.

7
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:14,310
今天的烧脑问题：一块太空石头，
Today's brain teaser: how can a space rock

8
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:17,020
凭什么亮成这样，还带绿光？
glow this bright, and green?

9
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:18,390
认识主角：小火，
Meet our hero, Blaze,

10
00:00:18,390 --> 00:00:20,730
一块在太空飘了很久的石头。
a rock that drifted in space for ages.

11
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:22,150
它绕着太阳跑，
It circled the Sun,

12
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:23,880
正好撞上了地球。
and ran right into Earth.

13
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:25,140
撞进来有多快？
How fast?

14
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:27,960
流星一般每秒十几到七十多公里。
Meteors hit at roughly 11 to 72 km per second.

15
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:29,230
每秒几十公里，
Tens of km per second:

16
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:31,150
比步枪子弹快几十倍。
dozens of times faster than a rifle bullet.

17
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:32,560
反转一：它发光，
Twist one:

18
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:34,270
主要不是因为摩擦。
it does not glow mainly from friction.

19
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:36,940
它太快了，前面的空气来不及让开，
It is so fast the air ahead cannot get out of the way,

20
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:38,400
被挤得越来越紧。
and gets squeezed tighter and tighter.

21
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:39,520
就像打气筒，
Like a bike pump:

22
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:41,320
压得越狠，筒越烫。
the harder you squeeze, the hotter it gets.

23
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:43,570
被压缩的空气烫到几千度，
The squeezed air reaches thousands of degrees,

24
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:45,930
把石头表面烤化、烤成气。
melting and vaporizing the rock's skin.

25
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:48,080
这团热气和空气一起发光，
That hot gas glows along with the air.

26
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:49,280
这就是流星。
That is a meteor.

27
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:50,680
反转二：为啥是绿的？
Twist two: why green?

28
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:52,340
颜色是原子的“指纹”。
Color is an atom's fingerprint.

29
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:54,220
每种原子被烤“兴奋”了，
Each kind of atom, when heated up,

30
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,370
只放出自己那几种颜色的光。
gives off only its own few colors.

31
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:58,080
就像烟花：钠是黄的，
Like fireworks: sodium is yellow,

32
00:00:58,080 --> 00:00:59,060
锶是红的。
strontium is red.

33
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:01,790
流星里的镁、镍，还有空气里的氧，
Magnesium and nickel in the rock, plus oxygen in the air,

34
00:01:01,790 --> 00:01:03,790
常被认为是绿光的来源。
are the usual suspects for green.

35
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:05,050
黄色常来自钠，
Yellow often comes from sodium,

36
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:07,510
红色常来自空气里的氮和氧。
red from nitrogen and oxygen in the air.

37
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,670
所以网友看到的绿、黄、红，
So the green, yellow and red people saw

38
00:01:09,670 --> 00:01:11,620
其实是一张化学成分表。
is really a chemistry report.

39
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:13,970
反转三：为啥北京、河北、山西
Twist three: how could Beijing, Hebei and Shanxi

40
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:15,430
都能看到同一颗？
all see the same one?

41
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:18,260
因为流星多半在七八十公里以上发光，
Meteors usually glow 70 km up or higher,

42
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,040
比飞机高差不多十倍。
about ten times higher than airliners.

43
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,730
站得越高，看得越远；
The higher you stand, the farther you see;

44
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:25,050
反过来，越高的东西，越多地方能看到它。
and the higher a thing is, the more places can see it.

45
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:28,400
新华社报道，山西阳泉的监测点也拍到了，
Xinhua says monitors in Yangquan, Shanxi caught it too,

46
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,810
我们估算离北京三百来公里。
about 300 km from Beijing by our estimate.

47
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:33,370
天文学上，比金星还亮的流星，
In astronomy, a meteor brighter than Venus

48
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:34,590
就叫火流星。
is called a fireball.

49
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:36,770
专家估计这颗“比较大个儿”，
Experts think this one was fairly big,

50
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:38,130
可能留下了陨石。
and may have dropped meteorites.

51
00:01:38,130 --> 00:01:40,410
大部分石头在半空就烧光了，
Most of the rock burns up high in the sky.

52
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:42,800
撑到地面的那一点才叫陨石。
The bits that reach the ground are meteorites.

53
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,560
最后那一段它不亮了：
The last stretch is dark:

54
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:47,840
空气把它刹得很慢，像普通石头一样掉。
the air brakes it until it just drops like a stone.

55
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,110
地球每天要挨多少太空石头？
How much space stuff hits Earth each day?

56
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:51,810
常见估计：几十吨。
A common estimate: tens of tons.

57
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:53,620
绝大多数比沙粒还小，
Almost all of it is smaller than sand grains,

58
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:55,000
你根本看不见。
far too small to see.

59
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,750
友情提示：目前还没有找到陨石的报道，
Fine print: no meteorite has been reported found yet.

60
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:59,240
“可能”只是可能。
Maybe means maybe.

61
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:00,880
真捡到可疑的石头，
If you find a suspicious rock,

62
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,730
先拍照、记位置，再联系天文馆。
photograph it, note the spot, and call a planetarium.

63
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:04,670
想看流星？
Want to see one?

64
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:08,190
找个远离灯光的地方，抬头多等一会儿。
Find a dark spot, look up, and be patient.

65
00:02:08,190 --> 00:02:10,320
所以小火凭什么亮成这样？
So why did Blaze glow so bright?

66
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,560
速度太快，把空气压烫了。
It was so fast it crushed the air red hot.

67
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:13,680
为啥带绿光？
Why green?

68
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:15,650
原子在报自己的名字。
The atoms were calling out their names.

69
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:17,990
一块石头在太空飘了几十亿年，
A rock that drifted for billions of years

70
00:02:17,990 --> 00:02:20,850
最后五秒，给三个省放了场烟花。
spent its last five seconds as fireworks for three provinces.

